@@FLAG_ROWS@@

None of these is a reliable speed win: differences of up to @@FLAG_SPREAD@@ percent are within the noise
of a network bound scan. ``--no-vendor-scan`` is the only one that helps consistently on a large tree
(@@NVS_REPO@@ @@NVS_DEFAULT@@ s to @@NVS_FLAG@@ s), since it skips the walk for vendored code. Use the flags for what they
change in the report, not for speed.

----

Memory and CPU
--------------

@@MEMORY@@ CPU time is small next to wall time for every language that waits on the network (Go at 100
dependencies: @@GO_CPU@@ s of CPU in @@GO_WALL@@ s), except Node, which starts one ``npm view`` process per
package (@@NODE_CPU@@ s of CPU in @@NODE_WALL@@ s at @@NODE_RUNG@@ direct dependencies).

.. _performance-unauthenticated:

Without a GitHub token
----------------------

@@UNAUTH_ROUNDS_CAP@@ rounds an hour apart, on @@UNAUTH_SUBJECTS@@, with no token.
A first run spends @@GH_MIN@@ GitHub requests on the license table (@@GH_MAX@@ when two analyzers run), so the
unauthenticated limit of 60 requests an hour allows about @@RUNS_PER_HOUR@@ first runs an hour from one address.
@@GH_REFUSED@@ A later run with a filled cache spends none. Set ``GITHUB_TOKEN`` on shared
CI runners, where many jobs share one address.

----

Compared with other tools
-------------------------

The same repositories were scanned with other tools. They do different jobs, so this is context,
not a leaderboard: speed mostly reflects whether a tool looks licenses up at all.

.. list-table::
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 16 12 12 14 46

   * - Tool
     - Repositories
     - Tool, median s
     - Feluda, same repositories, median s
     - What it does differently
@@TOOL_ROWS@@

Where a tool reported a license for a package, it agreed with Feluda in nearly every case once both
were written as SPDX ids (@@AGREEMENT@@). The remaining differences come from three
places: the installed version against the version in the manifest (``minipass``, ``fast-uri``), a
license file against registry metadata (``rison``, ``ieee754``), and free text license names.
OWASP dep-scan (needs a 42 GB vulnerability database) and LicenseFinder (runs ``bundle install``,
which builds native gem extensions) were not run.

----

.. _performance-limits:

Known limits
------------

What the study found Feluda does slowly today, in order of how much it costs:

#. **Registry answers are not cached.** A repeat run asks the registry again for every dependency it
   cannot read from disk, so a repeat run of Go, Python, R or C++ is at most @@REPEAT_MAX@@ times faster
   (.NET does better only because NuGet's own cache skips the restore). Go at @@GO_TOP_RUNG@@ dependencies
   sends @@GO_TOP_WARM_REQS@@ requests on a repeat run.
#. **Go, Python, .NET, R, C and C++ look dependencies up one at a time.** Rust, Node, Java and Ruby
   run in parallel. This is why Go costs about @@GO_PER_DEP@@ s per module, and why @@GO_DNF@@.
#. **Node starts one** ``npm view`` **process per package** it cannot read from ``node_modules``,
   including platform specific optional packages, at about @@NODE_PER_PKG_MIN@@ to @@NODE_PER_PKG_MAX@@ s each on a first run.
#. **No connect timeout.** A registry address that never answers costs the full 30 s request
   timeout, and Maven Central's search API does this for about half of its requests.
   ``dotnet list package`` has no timeout at all.
#. **What is reported depends on what is installed.** express reports @@EXPRESS_COLD_DEPS@@ dependencies without
   ``node_modules`` and @@EXPRESS_WARM_DEPS@@ with it; a Python project reports what uv resolves when uv is on
   ``PATH``. Scan the same way in CI and locally to compare reports.
#. **License text is kept as the registry wrote it** for many Maven, CRAN, PyPI and NuGet packages
   (for example "Apache License, Version 2.0" or "MIT + file LICENSE"), rather than as an SPDX id.
   SBOM output maps most of these.
#. **The OSI list is fetched on every run**, one request, and a failed fetch is silent.
